/* book Worked example: Q: For each change, say whether knock tendency increases or decreases and give the group and the reason: (a) compression ratio raised from 8 to 10; (b) spark advanced from 15° to 25° bTDC; (c) engine speed raised from 1500 to 3000 rev/min at the same load; (d) mixture changed from φ = 1.1 to φ = 0.85; (e) fuel changed from 91 to 95 RON.
   1. (a) Density factor. A higher compression ratio raises end-gas temperature and pressure, so τ shrinks: knock increases.
   2. (b) Density factor. Advanced spark puts the burn nearer TDC, so peak pressure and end-gas temperature rise: knock increases.
   3. (c) Time factor. More turbulence and faster flame, so the end gas waits less: knock decreases.
   4. (d) Composition factor. φ = 0.85 is lean (85 % of the stoichiometric fuel–air ratio), well away from the worst mixture at φ ≈ 1.1: knock decreases.
   5. (e) Composition factor. Higher octane lengthens τ: knock decreases.
   Answer: (a) and (b) increase knock; (c), (d) and (e) decrease it. */
