/* book exam traps: The CI delay is measured from the start of injection, not from TDC or from any spark; a CI engine has no spark. | Speed shortens the delay in milliseconds but lengthens it in crank degrees. Say which unit you mean. | Injection timing has an optimum near 10° to 15° bTDC: "retarding always shortens the delay" is wrong once injection moves past TDC. | A long delay enlarges the premixed stage, not the mixing-controlled one. | Cetane number is for diesel ignition quality; octane number is for petrol knock resistance. A high-cetane fuel has a low octane number. */
